hi there steve kaufman here to talk  about language learning and to talk  about something that i think is quite  important  and that is focus  how do we learn to focus we need to  focus in language learning uh i googled  focus i found lots of articles about  things we can do to improve our focus we  can meditate or or you know how do we  push distractions out of our head and  stuff like that  but i would like to suggest a different  approach and that is in order to focus  when language learning and learning a  language  don't focus  diversify all right  before i get into explaining what i mean  and and and referring a little bit to my  own language learning uh habits  i want to refer to two basic concepts  that at least i believe are important to  how the brain learns  the first comes from this book by  manfred spitzer  s-p-i-t-z-e-r a german neuroscientist  and this is a very interesting book  about learning you can google you can  find it on amazon and one of the things  he said in there that has stuck with me  is that first of all he explains how  obviously the brain learns through  experience by being exposed to  different things in life including  language  but that the brain requires  both repetition and novelty new things  in order to stay focused in order to  learn  if it just has repetition after a while  it learns less and less  robert bjork who is a professor of  psychology at the university of  california in los angeles  deals with the same subject from a  different perspective  and you can find his videos on youtube b  j o r k robert bjork  and his  main you know contribution at least to  my understanding of how we learn is this  concept of interleaving  that we are better off to  interleave to not try to  block learn something but rather to  learn this and then go and learn that  then come back to the first thing again  and that this strengthens our ability to  remember  okay so  novelty as well as repetition and  interleaving so how do we apply this to  language learning well if i look at my  own particular case  arabic so i have  been spending a lot of time with our  many stories at link i've listened to  some of them 30 40 50 times read them  many many times there's 60 stories  i have uploaded essie mill and i've gone  through a number of to those stories a  number of times i've done the  manual  modern  which is also uploaded to link  and then recently i started looking at  harrops  in fact  and i've got the audio for harp so i've  been listening to the audio for arabs i  even went to the defense language  institute site where you can find a lot  of old courses and there's one on arabic  i mean absolute brain dead repetition i  don't know how people could stay  in a full-time course like that but  again it's another kind of learning and  every time that you discover  words or  structures of the language that you have  perhaps learned before and forgotten and  then you find them again somewhere else  it's sort of a bit of repetition and yet  a bit of novelty there's a tremendous  sense of satisfaction in discovering  these things in a different context  so by not focusing just on one textbook  i find that i'm covering the same  material in different textbooks  and when i switch to another textbook  and then switch back to our source book  and switch back to the first one  i feel a great sense of satisfaction  whereas if i continually stay with the  same material after a while my  enthusiasm my interest  that sense of novelty is no longer there  and therefore my ability to focus  diminishes so  diversifying  the material you use  the approaches you use the things you do  actually improves your ability to focus  i could even take it a little further by  saying that  when i listen  i have to be doing something else doing  the dishes i mean that defense language  institute material is so brain dead that  i was doing it while taking inventory of  something at home for two hours but it  was kind of going in the background and  i pick it up because it's so repetitious  i i couldn't do that with something that  i had to try to really understand  but  when i listen i can't sit down  and deliberately try to focus on  listening  i have to be washing the dishes working  out doing something else  if i just sit there and try to focus on  what i'm listening to  i lose focus i can't do it now maybe  that's just me  so actually some degree of multitasking  now you got to be careful what you're  doing but certainly driving doing the  dishes working out walking running these  are all things that i can do while  listening  so  refreshing things diversifying into  other things then coming back to what  you were doing before  all of that helps me focus  and i think this applies to other  subjects it may not apply to you know i  don't know science maybe where you have  to learn  you know the basics and then you build  on that and build on that whereas  language is not really like that you're  getting it in different  forms from different sources and certain  things are sucking into place and other  things take longer to sock into place  and by exposing yourself to the language  in different situations it starts to  become  a part of you but i think a lot of um  you know history for example is like  that or the humanities is like that uh i  think uh it's important if you're  learning history to read three or four  books on the same  subject rather than trying to focus on  learning the one textbook i mean getting  back to language learning  typically in our school system there is  a textbook there is a curriculum the  kids are taught that this is what you  have to learn  they're reluctant to stray from the  curriculum  and yet the results are very poor at  least in in the canadian system the you  know english speaking school kids don't  learn to speak french very well even  though they stay with the curriculum  maybe  in our schools as i've said before we  should just allow kids to explore and  what we should measure  rather than performance on tests is how  many words have they read in the  language how many hours have they  listened to the language  if they're using link how many words  have they saved you know any of this  measurement of their activity  you know  diversified activities spread out over  different types of material that they  find interesting  so that they are achieving some  repetition in the language but at the  same time they are at least provided  some degree of novelty  so  you know  there are many distractions today  in learning a language language learning  has become much more efficient because  we can do it on our our you know  portable  tablets or  smartphones and so forth or on the  internet but of course those are also  places where there are lots of  distractions  and when we tend to want to  go off  check our twitter feed is when our  interest in the language is kind of  sagging so therefore in order to  better focus  stray a bit within  the language you're learning  in order to stay focused  i'm not going to address today the issue  of studying another language i have  stayed focused on arabic for the last  six seven months and  i may stray into farsi when we get farsi  many stories up at link and that seems  to be delayed and delayed  but it's still within the sort of arabic  writing system we'll see what happens  but  i i i seriously recommend that in order  to be better at staying focused  try to diversify what you're doing you  may find that you are more focused  thank you for listening bye for now  [Music]  you
 

